window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.1","title":"Sampling Variability and Sampling Distributions","goal":"Draw 1,000 samples from a skewed population and watch the sample means form a bell-shaped sampling distribution centred on the true mean.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Build a population","text":"A made-up population of 100,000 values, skewed to the right (a few large values), with a true mean near 30 and a true SD near 15, like the lesson's builder. The seed makes the numbers repeatable.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\npopulation = 15 + rng.exponential(15, size=100_000)\nmu = population.mean()\nsigma = population.std()\n\nprint(f'population mean (mu)   = {mu:.2f}')\nprint(f'population SD (sigma)  = {sigma:.2f}')","out":"population mean (mu)   = 30.05\npopulation SD (sigma)  = 15.08","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw 1,000 samples of size 30","text":"Each row below is one random sample of 30 people. We compute the mean of every row, so we end up with 1,000 different sample means. That pile of means is the sampling distribution.","code":"n = 30\nsamples = rng.choice(population, size=(1000, n))\nsample_means = samples.mean(axis=1)\n\nprint('first five sample means:', sample_means[:5].round(2))\nprint('number of sample means:', len(sample_means))","out":"first five sample means: [30.09 34.36 30.63 27.59 31.43]\nnumber of sample means: 1000","figs":[]},{"title":"Centre and spread","text":"The sample means should centre on mu, and their spread (the standard error) should be close to sigma divided by the square root of n.","code":"print(f'mean of the sample means = {sample_means.mean():.2f}  (mu = {mu:.2f})')\nprint(f'SD of the sample means    = {sample_means.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'theory sigma / sqrt(n)    = {sigma / np.sqrt(n):.2f}')","out":"mean of the sample means = 29.99  (mu = 30.05)\nSD of the sample means    = 2.83\ntheory sigma / sqrt(n)    = 2.75","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the sampling distribution","text":"A histogram shows the shape. Even though the population is skewed, the sample means pile up in a fairly symmetric bell around mu. The dashed line marks mu.","code":"plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.hist(sample_means, bins=30, color='steelblue', edgecolor='white')\nplt.axvline(mu, color='black', linestyle='--', label='population mean')\nplt.xlabel('sample mean (n = 30)')\nplt.ylabel('number of samples')\nplt.title('Sampling distribution of the mean')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":["4.1-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"set.seed(42)\npopulation <- 15 + rexp(100000, rate = 1/15)\nmeans <- replicate(1000, mean(sample(population, 30, replace = TRUE)))\nmean(population)\nmean(means)\nsd(means)\n15 / sqrt(30)\nhist(means, breaks = 30)","expect":"mean(population) about 30.05; mean(means) about 30; sd(means) about 2.8; 15 / sqrt(30) = 2.738 (Python: 30.05, 29.99, 2.83, 2.75)","note":"Random draws differ between R and Python, so simulated numbers are close but not identical; the theory value 15 / sqrt(30) = 2.74 is exact."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (with Random Number Functions), then Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives","syntax":"SET SEED=42.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP s = 1 TO 1000.\nCOMPUTE total = 0.\nLOOP i = 1 TO 30.\nCOMPUTE total = total + 15 + RV.EXP(1/15).\nEND LOOP.\nCOMPUTE xbar = total/30.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=xbar /STATISTICS=MEAN STDDEV.","note":"This draws each sample straight from the skewed model instead of a stored population, so the mean of xbar is near 30 and its SD near 2.7, close to but not exactly the Python numbers."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset seed 42\nset obs 1000\ngenerate xbar = 0\nforvalues i = 1/30 {\n    quietly replace xbar = xbar + (15 + rexponential(15)) / 30\n}\nsummarize xbar\ndisplay 15 / sqrt(30)\nhistogram xbar","note":"Each of the 1,000 rows gets the mean of 30 fresh draws; the Mean and Std. dev. of xbar should be near 30 and 2.7, close to but not identical to Python."},"try":["Change n to 100 and predict the new SD of the sample means (sigma divided by 10), then run it.","Change 1000 samples to 100 and see how much the histogram looks rougher.","Replace the exponential population with rng.uniform(0, 60, 100_000). Does the sampling distribution still look like a bell?"]});
